    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of the Igbo-Ukwu culture</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/igbo-people.jpg" alt="Igbo people" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Igbo people <span class="kid-tale-cr">Jerry Emeka Obi, CC BY-SA 4.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>More than 1,000 years ago, people lived at Igbo-Ukwu, in south-eastern Nigeria.</p><p>Its name means “Great Igbo”, and its finds are linked with the Nri-Igbo people.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/igbo-ukwu.jpg" alt="Igbo-Ukwu" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Igbo-Ukwu <span class="kid-tale-cr">Ochiwar, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>Their metalworkers cast bronze pots, crowns and ornaments covered in tiny, fine details.</p><p>Even small insects that landed on the surface were cast into some pieces.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nsukka.jpg" alt="Nsukka" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Nsukka <span class="kid-tale-cr">Damianp001, CC BY-SA 4.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>In the Nsukka region nearby, people had already made iron for more than 2,000 years.</p><p>Most of the metal for the bronzes came from old mines about 100 kilometres away.</p></li><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>Archaeologists found about 165,000 beads of glass, carnelian and other stone.</p><p>Some glass beads came through trade from as far as Egypt, Iran and Mesopotamia.</p></li><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>Isaiah Anozie found the first bronzes at his home and used some pots to water his goats.</p><p>Experts first doubted they were made there, but tests proved them local, and some are now in the British Museum.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-ukwu">Igbo-Ukwu</a> is a town in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> whose three excavated sites, a shrine, a burial chamber and a cache, held more than 700 objects of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, bronze and iron.</li><li>Its smiths cast bronzes of such fine detail that experts first doubted they were local; analysis showed that most of the metal came from mines about 100 kilometres away.</li><li>Glass <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> from as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> show that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-ukwu">Igbo-Ukwu</a> was tied into long-distance trade centuries before Europeans reached the coast.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Isaiah Anozie, who found the first bronzes while digging in his compound in 1938, did not know what they were: he gave some to friends and neighbours and used some of the vessels to water his goats.</p></aside>
    </section>

