    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 2000 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Iron is smelted at Lejja in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nsukka">Nsukka</a> region, about 100 kilometres east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-ukwu">Igbo-Ukwu</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">9th century CE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze">Bronzes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> and a rich burial are placed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-ukwu">Igbo-Ukwu</a>, according to the first radiocarbon dates</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1938</span><span class="rf-text">Isaiah Anozie finds bronzes while digging beside his home</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1946</span><span class="rf-text">The district officer J. O. Field hands the objects he had bought to the Nigerian Department of Antiquities</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1959–1964</span><span class="rf-text">Thurstan Shaw excavates <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-people">Igbo</a> Isaiah, Igbo Richard and Igbo Jonah</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1970</span><span class="rf-text">Shaw publishes radiocarbon dates placing the sites in the 9th century</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2022</span><span class="rf-text">New radiocarbon dates from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-people">Igbo</a> Isaiah fall between the 10th and 13th centuries</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-ukwu">Igbo-Ukwu</a> culture is an archaeologists’ name for the people who left three rich deposits in what is now the town of Igbo-Ukwu, “Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-people">Igbo</a>”, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anambra-state">Anambra State</a> in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>. The town stands about 366 metres above sea level on a sandstone ridge with little <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundwater-depletion">groundwater</a>, between the Aghomili River to the east and the Obizi River to the west. Metalworking in the region was far older: furnaces and slag from iron smelting have been dated to 2000 BCE at Lejja and 750 BCE at Opi, both in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nsukka">Nsukka</a> region about 100 kilometres to the east.</p><p>The first finds were made in 1938 by Isaiah Anozie while digging in his compound. J. O. Field, the British district officer, bought many of them, published them, and in 1946 handed them to the Nigerian Department of Antiquities; Kenneth Murray collected more in 1954. From 1959 to 1964 the archaeologist Thurstan Shaw excavated three areas: Igbo Isaiah around the first find, Igbo Richard and Igbo Jonah.</p><p>Shaw’s radiocarbon dates, published in 1970, placed the sites in the 9th century, around 850 CE. Newer dates from Igbo Isaiah, published by Daraojimba and colleagues in 2022, fall between the 10th and 13th centuries, and a textile from the same site was dated to about 1027–1180. These results suggest that Igbo-Ukwu may have been used over several centuries, and that some objects may be younger than once thought, though all of them come well before European contact with the coast.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="society">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Society</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The finds suggest an economy based on farming and trade. Excavations west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-people">Igbo</a> Richard in 2019 and 2021 yielded pottery, bronze, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and iron objects, iron slag, chalk, bone and palm kernel, and the earlier digs found iron swords, spearheads and razors. Igbo Jonah, the largest excavated area at 19 by 11 metres, produced the least material: its pits had been deliberately backfilled, and it yielded ritual pottery, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charcoal">charcoal</a>, animal bones and some bronzes.</p><p>Igbo Richard was a burial chamber holding the remains of several people, beaded wristlets and anklets, and a wooden stool associated with a single skeleton, possibly that of a priest-king; one body was adorned in what appears to be regalia. One of the bronzes is a small pendant in the shape of a chief’s head with ichi scarification marks on the face.</p><p>The town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-ukwu">Igbo-Ukwu</a> is described as the capital of the Kingdom of Nri from the 8th or 9th century, and the sites are associated with the Nri-Igbo, though how the deposits relate to that kingdom is not set out in detail. The finds also overturned a colonial-era view that such craft could not come from an acephalous, or egalitarian, society such as that of the Igbo.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Worldview</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The three sites are read as a shrine (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-people">Igbo</a> Isaiah), a burial chamber (Igbo Richard) and a cache (Igbo Jonah), and many of the bronzes are ritual vessels. From them archaeologists infer that by the 9th century the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-ukwu">Igbo-Ukwu</a> had a complex religious system. The burial at Igbo Richard, with its stool and what appears to be regalia, is read by some as that of a priest-king, but this too is interpretation.</p><p>The textiles found at Igbo Isaiah have been thought to hold religious or spiritual meaning, but the ethnographic and archaeological evidence for the meaning of bark-fibre cloth in the forest zone is thin, so this remains speculation. Much about the beliefs behind the objects is still unknown.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="arts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Arts &amp; Knowledge</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/igbo-ukwu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/igbo-ukwu.jpg" alt="Igbo-Ukwu" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/igbo-ukwu">Igbo-Ukwu</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bronze_ornamental_staff_head,_9th_century,_Igbo-Ukwu.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ochiwar, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The excavations recovered more than 700 high-quality objects of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, bronze and iron, about 165,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> of glass, carnelian and stone, pottery, textiles and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>. The bronzes were made by lost-wax casting, probably with latex rather than beeswax, which may explain their fine, filigree surface detail. The smiths did not use wire making, soldering or riveting, which suggests a tradition that developed on its own; small insects that seem to have landed on the surface were cast in one piece with the vessel. Complex objects were made in stages and joined by brazing or by casting linking sections, techniques not known elsewhere.</p><p>The alloy is unusual, with a high <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> content unlike that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> or other African bronze centres. About 85–90% of the ore came from old mines at Abakaliki, about 100 kilometres away, with a small share from a second source not yet identified; lead isotopes point to possible sources in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. The Grove Encyclopedia of Materials and Techniques in Art calls the bronzes “among the most inventive and technically accomplished bronzes ever made”, and Hugh Honour and John Fleming describe a water pot in a mesh of cast rope as “a virtuoso feat of cire perdue (lost wax) casting”.</p><p>The textiles were woven from Ficus bark fibre and raffia palm, with yarn 0.3–0.4 millimetres thick, an old weaving tradition of the Nigerian forest zone that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and wool have almost wholly replaced.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="exchange">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Contact &amp; Exchange</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bead"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bead.jpg" alt="Bead" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bead">Bead</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beads.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">pschemp, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> show long-distance trade. An analysis of 138 of them found that the most common type was soda-lime glass made with plant ash, probably from glass produced in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and carried along an eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> corridor by the 9th or 10th century. Some glass and carnelian beads have been traced to the workshops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fustat">Fustat</a> in Old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>. Other glass types have no known source yet, and Susan McIntosh argues that the possibility of trade across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> needs more research.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-ukwu">Igbo-Ukwu</a> is described as the centre of a trade system linking it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao">Gao</a> on the bend of the Niger and, through Gao, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. Its people traded with other African peoples as far as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> valley, while most of their metal came from the Abakaliki mines nearby.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/british-museum"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/british-museum.jpg" alt="British Museum" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:British_Museum_from_NE_2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ham, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The sources do not say how the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-ukwu">Igbo-Ukwu</a> culture ended. Its bronzes stand centuries before the other known bronzes of the region, and their quality first led scholars to imagine that they were made after contact with Europeans. Isotope analysis and radiocarbon dating proved them local and far older, and with that they undid the colonial-era assumption that such work could only come from places in touch with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Denis Williams called them “an exquisite explosion without antecedent or issue”, and Frank Willett compared their standard with that of Benvenuto Cellini five centuries later.</p><p>Five objects from Isaiah Anozie’s first finds are in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>: a small staff, a ram’s head, a large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manilla">manilla</a>, a crescent-shaped vessel and the pendant of a chief’s head. After a memorial for Thurstan Shaw in 2014, the Igwe of Igbo-Ukwu, Martin E. Ezeh, allowed new fieldwork, coordinated by Pamela Jane Smith Shaw in 2019 and 2021, which aims to widen the record of the ancient settlement in time and space.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-ukwu">Igbo-Ukwu</a> culture is known from three sites in a town of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>: a shrine, a burial chamber and a cache, first dated to the 9th century CE and possibly used for several centuries. Its smiths cast finely detailed lost-wax bronzes from nearby ores, and its people traded for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> that came from as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. The finds overturned colonial assumptions about early African art.</em></p>

